==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|thider}}, from {{inh|en|ang|þider}}, an alteration (probably by analogy with {{m|ang|hider||hither}}) of earlier {{m|ang|þæder|t=to there}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*þadrê}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈðɪðə/|/ˈθɪðə/|a=RP}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈðɪðɚ/|/ˈθɪðɚ/|a=GA}}
* {{audio|en|En-us-thither.ogg|Audio (GA)}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɪðə(ɹ)|s=2}}
* {{hyphenation|en|thi|ther}}

===Adverb===
{{en-adv|-}}

# {{lb|en|chiefly|literary|or|law|archaic}} To that [[place#Noun|place]].
#* {{RQ:King James Version|Genesis|19|20|passage=Behold now, this city is near to flee unto, and it is a little one: Oh, let me escape '''thither''' {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Boyle Sceptical Chymist|page=9|passage={{...}} Eleutherius, who thinking himself concern'd, because he brought me '''thither''' {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Defoe Tradesman|volume=I|letter=XXIII|chapter=Of the Inland Trade of England, Its Magnitude, and the Great Advantage It is to the Nation in General|page=390|passage=[A]ll thoſe goods, and a great deal of money in ſpecie, is return'd hither for and in ballance of our ovvn manufactures and merchandizes exported '''thither'''; {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Asbjornsen Braekstad Tales|page=280|passage=But the road left the river again; there were certainly twistings and turnings, as the old woman had said, for at one moment it wound hither and the next '''thither''', and at some places it was almost imperceptible.}}
#* {{RQ:Joyce Ulysses|passage=And there rises a shining palace whose crystal glittering roof is seen by mariners who traverse the extensive sea in barks built expressly for that purpose, and '''thither''' come all herds and fatlings and firstfruits of that land for O'Connell Fitzsimon takes toll of them, a chieftain descended from chieftains.}}
# {{lb|en|archaic}} To that point, end, or result.
#: {{ux|en|The argument tended '''thither'''.}}

====Usage notes====
* Still common in the expression "[[hither and thither]]".

